Z||000000||Avery Montembeault||02-20-2001||02:26 AM||||I reccently asked help and advice on fixing an 88 fiero I got for free. for those of you who dont remember, it had a busted timing gear, and I had never worked on an engine of this type. well, Im happy to say after spending the better part of the weekend tearing stuff apart, I got it all hooked back up and the engine is running beautifully. There are a few minor things I still need to fix in order to get it back in 100% running order. one is the brakes (no biggie). the second is the clutch. it shifts fine, but you really got to push the pedal waaaaay down to change gears, particularly in reverse..is there a way to adjust this? my final problem is there is the instrumanet panel. some of the guages look a little off, and the left directional indicator stays on, even when I havent turned the turn signal on. it flashes when I signal left, and the rear light does not stay on as if i was always going to make a left turn, liek I said, its just the panel light. Any ideas how to fix this?? <P>After all that work to get that engine out (cause the bolts were rusted in so tight I had to cut them and then heat things up with a torch to pop them) You have no idea how gratifying it was to hear that engine turn over for the first time. ||||reg||7
Z||000001||Oreif||02-20-2001||09:58 AM||||Congrats! <BR>The clutch is hydralic, you may need to bleed it (simular to bleeding brakes)<BR>As for the turn signal dash lamp being on, you have a turn signal light that isn't working. (open filiment) turn on the hazard lights and walk around the car and see which bulb is bad.<BR>Which gages look off? If the car was sitting for a while, some of the sensor connectors could have corrosion in them causing the gages to read higher.||||reg||1
